cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Yeah! Tortä - an Android DNS engine whose pillars must prove they are working"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Saimonokuma"
    website: "https://github.com/Nova-Violet-Role"
repository-code: "https://github.com/Nova-Violet-Role/Yeah-Torta"
license:
  - AGPL-3.0-or-later
  - EUPL-1.2
version: 1.0.0-alpha
date-released: 2026-08-01
abstract: >-
  An Android DNS engine built on the principle that no claim ships without an
  instrument that could have failed. Nine pillars - a datapath firewall, a
  content-addressed offline CDN, a resolution ladder, an adaptive congestion
  tuner, upstream rotation gated on answering rather than reachability,
  reversible privilege elevation, encrypted transport, a deny plane with
  provably distinguishable attribution labels, and a userspace tun forwarder -
  implemented in a Rust core of roughly 116k lines with a user interface
  compiled from Rust via Slint. Invariants that must hold for every input are
  proved in Lean 4 rather than sampled: 84 machine-checked theorems across five
  modules, zero sorry, zero native_decide, each re-verified by the Lean kernel
  through leanchecker and each mutation-tested so that a theorem no mutation can
  kill is labelled decorative rather than sold as a guarantee. The engine
  carries 1311 unit tests, and the whole product - both shared objects, the
  UniFFI bindings and both APKs - is built from source by GitHub Actions, with
  gates that assert the built library actually contains its pillar symbols. The
  project publishes its unproven claims alongside its proven ones. PRE-RELEASE.
